 A send that fails raises `RuntimeError` once the client has stopped retrying.
 Treat the alert as unsent and page the secondary by hand.
 
+`RuntimeError` is not proof the alert was unsent. The last of the three
+attempts can deliver the SMS and lose only the reply, and there is no further
+attempt to discover that. Paging the secondary by hand is still the right
+call, but expect that it sometimes doubles a message that did get through.
+
+## Duplicate alerts
+
+One alert can reach you more than once. Copies are identical in body and
+arrive close together: three attempts bounded by a 5s timeout and a 2s
+backoff put the whole burst inside about half a minute, and a measured
+timeout burst spans 14s. Identical messages arriving minutes apart are
+separate sends rather than copies of one.
+
+Treat a burst of identical messages as one alert, and open one incident.
+Repetition carries no information: it does not mean the condition is worse,
+more urgent, or spreading across hosts. Three copies and one copy mean the
+same thing.
+
+Copies come from the retry path. A retry cannot distinguish "Kestrel never got
+it" from "Kestrel sent the SMS and the reply back to us was lost", so in the
+second case the handset buzzes again. Every attempt within one `send` call
+carries one `Idempotency-Key`, which lets Kestrel collapse the retries into a
+single message. Whether the Kestrel account honors that header is unverified
+against production; if duplicates continue, check that first.
+
+Deduplication covers a single `send` call. A caller that invokes `send` twice
+for one alert produces two messages under two keys, and nothing in this client
+merges them.
+
+The failure worth worrying about at 3am is the opposite one. A quiet phone is
+not evidence that nothing is wrong.
+
